<p>Local-service advertising works best when the campaign is built around a real service area, a measurable enquiry and the capacity to answer quickly. A plumber covering Wakad needs a different setup from a consultant serving all of India. Before choosing keywords, document the locations you genuinely serve, opening hours, minimum job value, response time and the exact services that are profitable.</p>

<h2>Define a qualified conversion before spending</h2>
<p>Do not treat every click or WhatsApp tap as a business result. Choose primary conversions that represent intent: a completed quotation form, a connected phone call of a useful duration, or a booked appointment. Keep secondary actions such as viewing the contact page available for diagnosis, but do not let weak signals drive bidding. Google Ads supports separate conversion actions for website enquiries and calls, so reporting can distinguish them.</p>

<h2>Build small, intent-led ad groups</h2>
<p>Group keywords by service and customer need, not by every possible wording. A home-repair company might separate emergency repair, annual maintenance and installation because the urgency, landing page and commercial value differ. Add location language only when it reflects where the service is actually offered. Start with controlled match types, review actual queries, and expand only when lead quality supports it.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2472708/about-the-search-terms-report">Google Ads search terms report</a> shows the significant searches that triggered ads. Review it regularly to find irrelevant intent, useful negative keywords and wording customers use that the landing page does not yet answer.</p>

<h2>Use a landing page that completes the promise</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Repeat the specific service and location promised in the ad.</li>
    <li>Explain scope, exclusions, response time and the next step.</li>
    <li>Show relevant proof such as credentials, reviews or completed work.</li>
    <li>Use one primary action and keep the form proportionate to the enquiry.</li>
    <li>Test phone links, form delivery and the mobile layout before launch.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A practical weekly review</h2>
<p>Compare spend, search terms, conversion actions and sales feedback together. Pause queries that repeatedly create irrelevant enquiries; do not judge a keyword only by cheap clicks. Check whether calls were answered and whether forms received a timely response. For a physical location with active location assets, Google may also report local actions such as calls and direction requests, but those should be interpreted alongside actual customer outcomes.</p>

<h2>Launch checklist</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Confirm service areas and exclude locations you cannot serve.</li>
    <li>Set distinct conversion actions for forms and qualified calls.</li>
    <li>Match each ad group to the most relevant landing page.</li>
    <li>Add an initial negative-keyword list based on known irrelevant intent.</li>
    <li>Run a real test enquiry and confirm attribution and notification delivery.</li>
    <li>Review search terms and lead quality every week during the learning period.</li>
</ol>
<p>A good local campaign is not the one with the most traffic. It is the one that consistently connects the right local need with a service the business can deliver profitably.</p>

